  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1010 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nStarting the extended period Friday, high pressure continues to \nremain parked across the SW CONUS. Guidance suggests that a \nshortwave moves off of the northern Rockies. The better forcing is \ncurrently forecast to remain to the north which puts precipation \npotential a little in question but currently counties along and \nnorth of Highway 36 currently appears to have the relative \nhigher potential.\n\nInto the weekend a bit more pronounced trough is seen moving into \nthe area from the Intermountain West. This is forecast to shift the \nhigh further south across the southern Plains. This may lead to a \nbit more active pattern across the area. The question will be how \nfar south will the trough sag and if it will allow vorticity maxima \nto move across the area which currently appears to be the only \nforcing at this time as the better jet dynamics are currently \nforecast to remain off to the north. A similar pattern then may\ncontinue throughout next week as well but the high elongates \nsome and has some indications of shifting a little east. If this\ndoes occur then an increase in monsoonal moisture may develop \nas this would then become a classic pattern daily showers and \nstorms. \n\nAs for temperatures for the period. As long as the high pressure \nremains nearby the area then temperatures would remain in the upper \n80s to mid 90s. If cloud cover were to remain thick enough then \ntemperatures would have to be knocked down around 5-10 degrees but \nit will be dependent on precipitation coverage through the day.",
